# `DfoBuilder::with_max_evaluations`

[Back to `dfo::DfoBuilder`](../DfoBuilder.md)

Sets the objective-evaluation budget.

## Signature

```cpp
dfo::DfoBuilder dfo::DfoBuilder::with_max_evaluations(std::int64_t max_evaluations) const;
```

## What It Does

Sets `DfoOptions::max_evaluations`, the budget to use when each objective call is expensive. A positive value overrides the method's own limit. A method checks the budget between steps, so the reported count can exceed the budget slightly. Zero keeps the default.

## Parameters

`std::int64_t max_evaluations`
: The budget; 0 keeps the default.

## Returns

A new builder with the change; the original is untouched.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

A budget of 30 evaluations.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace dfo = pluss::subsp::math::optimization;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto f = [](const Series &x) { return (x - 3.0).square().sum(); };   // minimum 0 at (3, 3)

  const dfo::DfoResult r = dfo::DFO::build(pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0})).with_objective(f).with_max_evaluations(30).solve();
  out.writeln(dfo::to_string(r.status), ", evaluations =", r.diagnostics.evaluations);
}
```

### Example 2

Quality per budget. With 50 evaluations the run is cut short; with 200 or 800 the simplex converges on its own after the same number of evaluations, so the extra budget is not used.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace dfo = pluss::subsp::math::optimization;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto rosenbrock = [](const Series &x) {
    const Series a = x.select(0, 0), b = x.select(0, 1);
    return (b - a * a).square() * 100.0 + (1.0 - a).square();
  };

  for (const std::int64_t n : {50, 200, 800}) {
    const dfo::DfoResult r = dfo::DFO::build(pluss::series::make({-1.2, 1.0})).with_objective(rosenbrock).with_max_evaluations(n).solve();
    out.writeln("budget", n, ": evaluations =", r.diagnostics.evaluations, ", minimum =", r.minimum);
  }
}
```

### Example 3

The budget on a swarm. The population is evaluated each generation, so the count moves in steps of the population size.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace dfo = pluss::subsp::math::optimization;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto f = [](const Series &x) { return (x - pluss::series::make({1.0, -2.0, 0.5, 3.0})).square().sum(); };   // minimum 0 at (1, -2, 0.5, 3)

  const dfo::DfoResult r = dfo::DFO::build(pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0})).with_objective(f)
                               .with_solver(dfo::DfoSolverKind::ParticleSwarm).with_max_evaluations(1000).solve();
  out.writeln(dfo::to_string(r.status), ", evaluations =", r.diagnostics.evaluations, ", generations =", r.diagnostics.iterations);
}
```

